Key Findings

1. Split is unanimously detected – all AIs recognised the root/shoot bifurcation as a clear qualitative asymmetry. The refined definition works well for this.
2. Tension is debated – about half the AIs saw tension (opposing tropisms), the other half saw coordinated growth without conflict. This reflects a genuine ambiguity in the process: tropisms are directional gradients, but they are not necessarily opposing forces in a conflict sense. The refined definition may need to clarify whether a simple polarity counts as tension.
3. Failed Merge is correctly absent – all AIs agreed no attempt to reintegrate root and shoot. Excellent.
4. Scar assignment is inconsistent – two AIs (Gemini, Google‑Gemini) assigned scars (cotyledons, side roots) despite no failed merge. Under the refined definition, a scar requires a failed merge residue. This indicates those AIs did not follow the definition strictly. Others correctly said None.
5. Decay is unanimously absent – all AIs correctly recognised no loss of structure. Good.

